# ---- GOLDEN FIXTURE (bridge tile) ----
# resetMode=loadvm -> restore the INTERNAL 'golden' snapshot: X (-nocursor) +
# MAME 0.289 `bbcb -tube arm` sitting INSIDE ARM BBC Basic V 1.00, running on
# the ARM second processor. Unlike bbcmicro this fixture is NOT the machine's
# untouched first screen: the ARM Evaluation System has no language of its own,
# so two commands are baked in before the snapshot and deliberately left on
# screen as the prompt's provenance.
SH_RESET_MODE=loadvm
SH_GOLDEN_SNAPSHOT=golden
SH_FIXTURE_DESC=Acorn ARM Evaluation System (1986) — the first ARM product ever sold — sitting at the prompt of ARM BBC Basic V version 1.00, running ON the ARM second processor. The screen reads "ARM Second Processor 4096K / Acorn ADFS / BASIC", then the two supervisor lines that got it there, "A* *LIB $" and "A* AB", then the ARM BASIC banner and a ">" prompt. Those two A* lines are BAKED INTO the fixture, not typed by a visitor: the board has no operating system of its own, the language comes off Disc 3 of the ARM Evaluation System floppy set as $.AB, and the ADFS library is "Unset" on a cold boot so *LIB $ is required before AB resolves at all (both AB and *AB answer "No directory (169)" without it). They are left visible because they ARE the exhibit — a 1986 co-processor loading its language off a floppy while the 6502 host is demoted to a terminal and a disc controller. Drawn by the same purpose-built MAME 0.289 `bbcb` the bbcmicro tile ships, with `-tube arm`, the Acorn 1770 disc interface (the ADFS .adl discs are double density; the 8271 the bbcmicro tile ships cannot read them) and Acorn ADFS 1.30 in sideways socket 3 — socket 1 kills the Tube outright and the banner falls back to a plain "BBC Computer 32K", i.e. a bbcmicro duplicate. Fullscreen with aspect correction on an 800x600 X root; -artwork_crop removes the driver's keyboard-LED strip. The whole frame contains exactly three colours: black, white, and the pure #0000FF of the reverse-video A* supervisor field. CAPS LOCK is on at reset, so unshifted letters arrive upper case, which is what BASIC's tokeniser needs. Keyboard-only exhibit; no pointer device; sound -> ALSA -> AC97.

# ---- KEYBOARD PACING (the exhibit's only input surface) ----
# Identical to bbcmicro's, and for the same reasons: same emulator, same MOS,
# same 50 Hz machine, same host. Playbook 5.1's two-frame floor is 40/40; the
# fleet ships 80/80 because host scheduling stalls on this box (30+ emulators)
# eat characters at the floor. Measured here: the registry demoProgram's 78
# characters go out in 14.4-15 s with nothing dropped. One character was lost
# in one of three ad-hoc type-ins under load average ~6 - the same residual the
# vic20 notes record, a property of the box and not of the emulated machine.
# Re-bisect with scripts/dev/emu-key-pacing-bisect.py if it ever gets worse.
SH_KEY_MIN_HOLD_MS=80
SH_KEY_MIN_GAP_MS=80
# THIRTEEN CHARACTERS SIT ON DIFFERENT KEYS THAN ON A US PC - bbcmicro's map,
# unchanged, because this is the same MOS and the same keyboard matrix. The
# ones this exhibit's listing hits are `"` and `%`. Value format: guest:host
# pairs; kept in lockstep with keyboard.charMap in registry/tiles/armeval.json.
SH_KEY_MAP=":@,':&,&:^,(:*,):(,=:_,@:\,+:%3A,^:=,~:+,_:`,%3A:',*:"
